29
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.833
Now let's look at how we can build
a simple validator that uses a combination

30
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:29.800
of techniques to detect
if there's any mentions of competitors in

31
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.300
any LLM text.

32
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.500
When you think about this problem,

33
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.500
you actually might think
that you can do a simple word match,

34
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:39.100
but a lot of companies are actually
referred to by multiple names.

35
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.600
So for example,
you can think of GMC versus JPMorgan.

36
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.600
And it's typically pretty hard
to enumerate all of the common names

37
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.600
that a competitor
might be referred to as.

38
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:54.300
So what we end up doing is building this
cascading approach to

39
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.600
detecting when a competitor is mentioned.

40
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.633
First, we check for an exact

41
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:01.700
match, and if we find an exact match

42
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.233
to one of our competitor names,
then we immediately fail the validator.

43
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.433
If we don't find an exact match,
however, we then go on to named Entity

44
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:12.900
Recognition, where we extract
all of the named entities that are

45
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.000
mentioned in any given text to us.

46
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.600
And then we try to see

47
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.033
if the vector embeddings of those named entities

48
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:22.633
are similar
to the embeddings of

49
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:24.400
our known competitors.

50
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.400
So the idea here would be
the JPMC would have a very close

51
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:29.700
embedding as that of JPMorgan.

52
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.500
If we notice this behavior

53
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.233
and the similarities are greater
than some threshold, then we again fail.

54
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:39.000
Depending on whatever
competitors are detected.

55
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
And if not, then we end up passing

56
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:41.800
and determined

57
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.500
that there's no mentions of competitors
in any text.

58
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.000
As we're setting this out,
we're going to import a number of

59
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.500
